unmask-release
==============

This package configures your system to use the unmask package repository
hosted at https://unmask.sh/dl/.

Files installed:

  RHEL / Rocky / AlmaLinux:
    /etc/pki/rpm-gpg/RPM-GPG-KEY-unmask          GPG public signing key
    /etc/yum.repos.d/unmask.repo                  yum/dnf repository definition

  Debian / Ubuntu:
    /etc/apt/keyrings/unmask.gpg                  GPG public signing key
    /etc/apt/sources.list.d/unmask.sources        apt repository (deb-822)

  Alpine:
    /etc/apk/keys/unmask-release@unmask.sh.rsa.pub  RSA public signing key
    /etc/apk/repositories                           apk repository (one line appended)

After installing this package you can install unmask with:

    dnf install unmask                            # RHEL/Rocky/AlmaLinux
    apt update && apt install unmask              # Debian/Ubuntu
    apk update && apk add unmask                  # Alpine

To verify the key fingerprint outside of this package, see:
    https://unmask.sh/keys/

Project: https://unmask.sh/
License: Apache-2.0

Trying a pre-release build
--------------------------
When a fix is published for confirmation it goes to the testing channel first.
That channel is configured by this package but never active on its own: an
ordinary update cannot reach it, and nothing has to be undone afterwards.

  RHEL / Rocky / Alma / openSUSE
      sudo dnf --enablerepo=unmask-testing update unmask

  Debian / Ubuntu
      sudo apt update
      sudo apt install unmask=<version>      # the version you were given

  Alpine
      sudo apk add --repository https://unmask.sh/dl/testing/apk/main unmask

The signing key is the same one this package installed, so there is nothing
else to set up.

Once the fix is confirmed, the identical file is copied into the stable
channel -- not rebuilt -- so the build you confirmed is the build that ships.
Your next ordinary update moves you back onto stable by itself.
